WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.540
Every action potential lets potassium out of the neuron.

2
00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:11.280
When a small patch of brain fires hard, a lot of potassium leaks out.

3
00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:13.940
The space between brain cells is narrow.

4
00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:16.240
So the potassium can build up there.

5
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.900
A neuron's resting voltage depends largely on potassium.

6
00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:21.680
Something has to put it back.

7
00:00:22.633 --> 00:00:26.793
Take an illustrative neuron with 140 mM potassium inside.

8
00:00:27.353 --> 00:00:30.093
At rest, 3 mM potassium sits outside.

9
00:00:30.793 --> 00:00:33.453
EK is then −102.6 mV.

10
00:00:33.973 --> 00:00:37.573
In an extreme case, suppose a small space reaches 12 mM.

11
00:00:38.253 --> 00:00:41.213
EK becomes −65.6 mV.

12
00:00:41.593 --> 00:00:43.953
That is 37.0 mV more positive.

13
00:00:44.553 --> 00:00:48.853
A real neuron's resting voltage moves less, but in the same direction, toward

14
00:00:48.853 --> 00:00:49.853
threshold.

15
00:00:50.967 --> 00:00:54.367
Astrocytes, a kind of glial cell, do a large share of that work.

16
00:00:54.907 --> 00:00:56.067
They use spatial buffering.

17
00:00:56.667 --> 00:01:00.287
Potassium enters an astrocyte where the outside concentration is high.

18
00:01:00.867 --> 00:01:05.107
It travels through that astrocyte, and through the network of astrocytes joined to

19
00:01:05.107 --> 00:01:06.147
it by gap junctions.

20
00:01:06.807 --> 00:01:08.727
It leaves where the concentration is low.

21
00:01:09.407 --> 00:01:12.967
Think of a sponge that soaks up a spill at one end and wrings out at the other.

22
00:01:14.017 --> 00:01:20.417
In this model the entry channel is Kir4.1, an inwardly rectifying potassium channel.

23
00:01:21.217 --> 00:01:26.117
Separately, sodium-potassium ATPases in glia and neurons take potassium up.

24
00:01:26.817 --> 00:01:29.657
Redistribution and net uptake are distinct mechanisms.

25
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:33.860
Ken McCarthy's laboratory tested the channel with genetics.

26
00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:37.560
They used a conditional knockout driven by a glial promoter.

27
00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:41.800
Djukic and colleagues deleted Kir4.1 from glia.

28
00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:47.960
In hippocampal slices from mice 15 to 20 days old, CA1 astrocytes took up potassium

29
00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:53.160
poorly. The deletion began in development, and it also reached oligodendrocytes.

30
00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:56.860
So the result cannot be pinned on mature astrocytes alone.

31
00:01:57.933 --> 00:02:03.453
Keep this rule. When neurons fire hard, potassium can build up outside them, and EK

32
00:02:03.453 --> 00:02:04.673
becomes more positive.

33
00:02:05.373 --> 00:02:09.353
The real resting voltage moves the same way, toward threshold, but less.

34
00:02:10.453 --> 00:02:14.073
Astrocytes pass that potassium through their network, from where it is high to where

35
00:02:14.073 --> 00:02:16.053
it is low. That is redistribution.

36
00:02:17.013 --> 00:02:20.613
Net uptake by the sodium-potassium ATPase is a separate mechanism.
